  "findings": {
    "medium_gpu_wall": "M2 and M4 pinned the 32 GiB GV100 at 32,482 MiB and did not complete. The medium padded box needs 3*M^3 complex values = 64.9 GB of unified working set, about 2x the device. M2 reached 51.8 GB of host RSS; the earlier legacy-CLI 2-rank run was observed for more than 6 h without producing a checkpoint.",
    "r2_large_slab_corruption": "The 2-rank reduced-medium runs (R2, R2b) completed but produced non-physical field values (E_x max 4.43e10 vs 5.01e-2 at 4/8 ranks); the values were identical in both runs. The 4- and 8-rank runs agree to 5.2e-10 and the 2-rank small case agrees with the 1-rank reference to 2.6e-10. This points at the 13 GiB/rank device-memory pressure on the 32 GiB card, not at the checkpoint gather or the 1/4/8-rank compute. Treat 2 ranks at this per-rank slab size as unsafe until the vendor-workspace failure is surfaced.",
    "replicated_mode": "The medium with --fft-mode replicated is rejected earlier by the per-rank cap (limit 268435456), because only a distributed context scales the cap; replicated would need ~64.9 GB per rank.",
    "r4_correctness": "R4 has no 1-rank reference because 540.7M padded nodes exceed the single-rank cap. It is checked by rank-count invariance against R8 (5.2e-10) and by the small-case 1-rank reference (S4 vs S1, 2.6e-10); its field magnitudes match the small-case physics."
  },
  "conclusion": "The --config path now scales the per-rank padded-node cap by the rank count before makeGrid, so a multi-rank TOML run above 2^28 padded nodes is accepted. The verified working large domain on this single 32 GiB GV100 is the reduced medium 440x320x480 at 2e-7 m: 540.7M padded nodes at 4 ranks in 85 s, 6.5 GiB GPU per rank (21.25 GiB total host peak), 2.42 GB checkpoint, 5.2e-10 rank-count agreement. The full medium 440x800x480 needs 3*M^3*16 B ~ 64.9 GB of unified working set and pins the 32 GiB device at 2 and 4 ranks; it does not complete. The first wall on this node is the GPU, not the 125 GiB host RAM."
}
